WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
Good morning John, give me four minutes

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:09.360
so that we can have a legit conversation about a warmer Earth, and what that means for actual weather.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.780
The kind of conversation that we never get to have because it doesn't fit into a 15-second soundbite.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.200
Here's what we all need to get, and I don't think it's anybody's fault

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.660
for not getting this. When we talk about the effect that a warmer Earth will have on the weather.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.420
We're not talking about what's gonna happen.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.280
We're talking about what's probably gonna happen, or more properly, the probability of what will happen.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.380
We are never going to know how many big storms are gonna hit the U.S. in any given hurricane season,

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:41.040
but we can take a look over the last 50 years and see how many major storms formed per year.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:41.879
And I'm doing that right now--

00:00:41.879 --> 00:00:47.999
I'm putting a dot for every year; most of the years had two storms, some of them had five, some have had four, and some had zero.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.430
That year with seven storms there, that was 2005, the year that Hurricane Katrina hit New Orleans. Alright, yeah, you see

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.090
what happened here. There is a curve.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.180
This is a probability distribution,

00:00:57.399 --> 00:01:03.869
and it is always how we should be thinking about how a changing climate is affecting weather today,

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:11.189
now, this year--not the dots, the curve. Every year at the end of the hurricane season, this probability curve

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:18.270
collapses into just another dot. It is no longer a set of things that could happen, it becomes a very specific thing that did happen.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:24.270
Last year it collapsed into four major storms, the year before that two, the year before that two again, and the year before that zero.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:28.110
Oh, do we have a trend happening here? No, no,

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:31.559
no, this is not a trend! Every year the curve runs itself again,

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.909
and it might collapse down to zero or it might collapse out to seven. Like, look at the dots!

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:41.699
Obviously, no one dot on this curve is important, and also the curve is saying that all this stuff happens. Everything

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:42.939
that's in here is well

00:01:42.939 --> 00:01:49.169
inside of the realm of normality. The number of serious storms from year to year can jump dramatically while being the effect of the same

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:54.570
probability curve. A warmer planet doesn't take you from this, to this, because global energy in the system

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:57.869
isn't the only important variable. It takes you from this,

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.629
to this. Something important is changing, and that's shifting the curve. When that new curve

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:07.468
collapses into its reality dot, that might still be at zero,

00:02:07.469 --> 00:02:12.089
it's just less likely than it used to be. And years like 2005 will still be rare,

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.270
they will just be less rare. And if there are more strong storms,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.800
there's a higher likelihood that they'll end up hitting in the worst places

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:26.020
possible, as Harvey did, as Katrina did. Homes that used to be considered safe might now find it difficult to get insurance because no one

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:26.830
understands

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:33.570
probability curves like the people who work at insurance companies. And these probability curves are what we use to determine whether a flood has, like,

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:38.160
a 1 in 100 chance of happening or a 1 in 50 chance of happening, and as that changes

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.480
those "hundred-year floods"

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.980
start to not be "hundred-year floods". And the curve can shift before

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:48.839
we're aware of it. Like, with this, we only get one new dot a year,

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.600
which is why we create computer models-- so that we can run

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.529
hurricane seasons over and over and over again with slightly changed

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:02.070
variables to see what will happen. And what we see with more energy in the global system is the curves shifting towards stronger,

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:07.020
wetter storms. The more carbon we release into the atmosphere the more these curves shift,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.749
and that is the future that we have to accept, and

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:15.900
the one that we have to prepare for. And also, the one it's good to try and understand a little bit, so thanks for

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.360
taking four minutes to do that. John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:23.340
That took substantially less than four minutes. I'm quite proud of myself. So here

